+**The loan-offer card.** `/advisory` draws an `OfferCheck` card (`components/OfferCheck.jsx`;
+the sums are in `data/loanOffer.js` and the sentences in `i18n/offerLines.js`): the reader says
+how a loan offer was told, types what they were told, and the tab works out what they get in
+hand, what they pay back in all and what it costs a year, beside the loan the rules priced and
+the same money at the rate the app assumes for its own loans (12% a year, read from the result).
+It runs in the tab: nothing is sent, nothing is stored, no model is involved, and print leaves
+the card out. It says what an offer costs and never calls one illegal, too high or unfair. It has
+two Node test files, a list of words it may not use, a Python reference and one browser gate.
+
+`offer-lib-test` (58 tests, `node --test`, no browser, no network) holds the sums. The yearly
+cost is checked three ways: against `offer_reference.py`, which finds it by another road (60-digit
+`Decimal` arithmetic, a loop that adds up the discounted payments one by one and 400 halvings,
+where the module uses a closed form (`expm1` and `log1p`) and 200 halvings), so the two cannot share
+a mistake; against the equation it was solved from, over a sweep of more than 1,000 offers; and
+against properties a right answer has (the same payments cost more a year the oftener they fall,
+doubling the loan and the payment changes nothing, a bigger payment is a dearer offer, payments
+that add up to the money in hand cost nothing extra, no figure is ever NaN or infinite). It also
+pins what is a blank and what is a mistake, the caps (₹100 crore, ten years of payments, 120
+months, a rate of 100), the half rupee that goes up even where floating point would drop it, and
+which of the priced loans is shown. `offer-lines-test` (48 tests) holds the sentences: each status
+gives the lines it should and no others, a mistake names its limit, every sentence exists in all
+8 languages with the placeholders the module fills, and none uses a word of `offer-banned.mjs`.
+That file is the one list of the words the card may not use (for English `illegal`, `too high`,
+`cheat`, `should`, `avoid` and the like; shorter lists for the other 7 languages), matched from the
+start of a word, and the gate reads it too. It is a guard against a slip and not a proof that a
+sentence cannot mislead; the tests check that it can fire.
+
+`offer-ui.mjs` needs Vite on 5173 and nothing else: no backend and no model. The result page is
+seeded (version 6) from the saved responses `case_empty.json` (the rules priced part of the gap),
+`case_good.json` (the whole gap), `case_green.json` (no gap) and `case_pressure.json` (no room),
+with copies of the last for the other two reasons no loan is priced, and two made from
+`case_good` (one saved without the loan options, one whose 60-month loan is the largest the
+schedule can price). It exits 2 if one of the four files is missing or is not what its name says.
+Every figure the page must print is written down in the gate, and the totals and yearly costs come
+from `offer_reference.py`, so a sum that is wrong in the app and in its own test is still caught;
+the words come from the dictionary itself. 74 page loads and 13,400 checks in 12 scenarios: the
+card appears empty, in all 8 languages, in Simple and in Detailed view (O1); the figures of every
+kind of offer: three frequencies, a cost, the same, less, the comparison more, less and the same,
+a half rupee and the largest numbers (O2); every mistake a box can hold and its message, the edge
+of what a box takes, and forty nines typed into every box (O3); the loan the rules priced, for
+each of the six statuses a response has, for the largest loan the schedule can price and for a
+response saved without the loan options (O4); a whole sitting sends and stores nothing, away to
+`/analysis` and back by the app's own links, and a reload (O5); results in the 8 languages (O6);
+layout at 320 px with the largest text in the dark in all 8 languages, at 390 px, on a desktop,
+and beside the largest priced loan (O7); what was typed for one way of telling an offer stays
+with it (O8); typing one digit at a time, backspacing, and an empty box (O9); a language switch
+with a result shown (O10); print (O11); and that the card is on no other page (O12). The script's
+header lists its 27 finding kinds. Port 9383, profile `screenshots/verify/edge-profile-offer`,
