+again. The priced loan has four figures; the widest of them in each window:
+
+| Window | Widest figure | The row it sits in | Lines, with the class and without |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 320 px, largest text, dark | 145.83 px | 214.25 px | 1 and 1, all four figures |
+| 390 px, smallest text | 116.25 px | 291.94 px | 1 and 1, all four figures |
+| 1280 px | 123.66 px | 1,072 px | 1 and 1, all four figures |
+
+By the rules of flex layout and not by a measurement: each row is `flex flex-wrap`, so a figure that
+does not fit beside its label moves to a line of its own, and the figure's computed `overflow-wrap`
+is `anywhere` (read in the page), so only a figure wider than the whole row would break inside the
+number. No window the gate opens holds a figure that wide. That is an argument that the defect
+cannot show in what the gate opens, not a proof for narrower windows, larger figures, other
+languages or other fonts. The class stays.
+
+**`C39` survived.** It removes `key={mode}` from the grid of boxes, so the grid is no longer
+rebuilt when the way of telling the offer changes. By reading the code and not by a test: only the
+first two boxes sit at the same place in both ways with the same component, and each takes its
+value from what was typed for the way shown (O8 checks that), so the reader sees the same thing;
+the third and fourth differ in type between the ways and are rebuilt either way. What the gate
+cannot see is how a screen reader treats an error message that stays on the page while its words
+change, and it does not look at what the browser holds inside an input box. Untested. The key
+stays.
+
